{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\nouicompat\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa120\f0\fs22 {\b\fs32 Prescriptive Specification -- CMA Process Edition}\par CMA Residence -- Birmingham, Alabama -- 2026 -- Issue 1.0\par\par {\i This specification governs any residence designed by CMA and built under CMA's construction administration. It is incorporated by reference into the construction drawings and into the owner-builder agreement. Where this specification, the drawings, and the schedules conflict, the more stringent requirement applies.}\par\par {\b\fs26 01  General Conditions & Scope of Drawings}\par {\b Authority of documents.}  The construction drawings, schedules, and this specification together constitute the contract documents. CMA is the architect of record and will provide construction administration consistent with AIA B101 / B105 as modified by the owner-architect agreement.\par {\b Architect's role.}  CMA will conduct site visits at intervals appropriate to the stage of the work, review submittals, respond to RFIs, evaluate change proposals, certify payment, and conduct substantial- and final-completion walks. CMA does not direct or control means, methods, sequencing, or safety, and is not responsible for the contractor's failure to perform the work in accordance with the contract documents.\par {\b Communication protocol.}  All formal direction, RFIs, submittals, and change requests shall be transmitted in writing through the architect-of-record. Verbal field instructions are not contract direction unless reduced to writing within forty-eight hours.\par \par {\b\fs26 02  Drawing Interpretation & Precedence}\par {\b Order of precedence.}  Where conflict exists, the more stringent requirement applies. Among themselves, the documents are read in this order: signed change orders; addenda; large-scale details; small-scale details and elevations; plans; schedules; this specification. Written dimensions govern over scaled measurements. Do not scale drawings.\par {\b Coverage.}  Items shown on one drawing and not another are required as if shown on all. MEP layouts are diagrammatic; the contractor is responsible for coordinated routing that respects framing, finishes, and ceiling heights shown.\par {\b Discrepancies.}  The contractor shall notify the architect in writing of any conflict, omission, or ambiguity before proceeding. Work installed in conflict with the documents will be removed and replaced at the contractor's expense.\par \par {\b\fs26 03  Site Documentation & Existing Conditions}\par {\b Field verification.}  The contractor shall verify all existing conditions, dimensions, elevations, setbacks, easements, and utility locations before excavation, fabrication, or ordering, and shall promptly notify the architect in writing of any discrepancy.\par {\b Records on site.}  The contractor shall maintain on site at all times a current set of stamped drawings, this specification, the project schedule, the submittal log, and the RFI log.\par {\b Concealed work photo log.}  The contractor shall photograph all framing, MEP, waterproofing, and flashing work before it is concealed and transmit those photographs to the architect weekly.\par {\b As-builts.}  Deviations shall be redlined on a dedicated record set and delivered to the architect at substantial completion.\par \par {\b\fs26 04  Contractor Qualifications & Licensing}\par {\b Contractor.}  The contractor shall be properly licensed by the State of Alabama Home Builders Licensure Board (Alabama Code \u167?34-14A), registered in the project's jurisdiction, and shall employ a full-time on-site superintendent with a minimum of five years' experience in custom residential work of comparable scale and detail. The contractor shall carry general liability and builder's-risk insurance naming the owner and architect as additional insured.\par {\b Specialized trades.}  Mechanical, electrical, plumbing, and gas-fitting work shall be performed by trades licensed by the Alabama Plumbers and Gas Fitters Examining Board, the Alabama Electrical Contractors Board, and the Alabama Board of HVAC Contractors as applicable. Subcontractors for masonry, roofing, plaster/stucco, millwork, and finish carpentry shall be similarly qualified and approved by the architect before commencement.\par \par {\b\fs26 05  Submittals & Approval Timelines}\par {\b Required submittals.}  Prior to fabrication or order, the contractor shall submit, for the architect's review and stamp: shop drawings for cabinetry, millwork, stairs, railings, exterior trim assemblies, doors, windows, roof and floor framing, and structural steel; physical samples of all exterior materials, including masonry, mortar, stucco, roofing, siding, paint, and stain; and product data for any item where a model, finish, or color must be selected.\par {\b Turnaround.}  The architect shall be allowed ten working days for first review and five working days for re-submittal review. The contractor shall plan the schedule accordingly. Fabrication or ordering before written approval is at the contractor's sole risk.\par {\b Submittal log.}  The contractor shall maintain a running log with dates, status, and revision numbers, and shall transmit the log to the architect with each pay application.\par \par {\b\fs26 06  Material Standards & Substitutions}\par {\b Standard quality.}  All materials shall be new, unused, of first quality, and free of defects.\par {\b CMA standing requirements.}  Half-round copper gutters and round copper downspouts. Copper flashing at all roof transitions, valleys, drip edges, pitch breaks, chimneys, beam caps, and roof-to-wall conditions. Copper or lead-coated copper pans at through-wall and shower conditions. Stainless steel or silicon-bronze fasteners at all exterior wood, brick mold, porches, decks, rafter tails, and any concealed fastening in contact with treated lumber. No exposed galvanized fasteners on visible exterior surfaces.\par {\b Wood -- exterior.}  Exterior trim, rafter tails, fascia, beadboard, and porch ceilings shall be a clear-grade rot-resistant species (Western Red Cedar, Spanish Cedar, Cypress, or Sapele); finger-jointed material is not permitted on the exterior.\par {\b Wood -- interior.}  Interior trim and millwork shall be solid wood per the drawings -- paint-grade poplar or stain-grade species. MDF only where expressly noted; no MDF in baths, kitchens, or wet areas.\par {\b Substitutions.}  No substitution shall be made without the architect's prior written approval. Substitution requests shall include physical samples, full technical data, and a written statement that the substitution equals or exceeds the specified item in performance, durability, and appearance. The architect's review of a substitution does not waive any other requirement of the contract documents.\par \par {\b\fs26 07  Workmanship Standards}\par {\b Standard of work.}  All work shall be performed by tradesmen skilled and experienced in their trade, in a manner consistent with the highest standard of residential custom construction. Lines true, surfaces plumb and level, joints tight and consistent, reveals uniform.\par {\b Trim and millwork.}  Trim returns, miters, and scribes hand-fit; caulk shall not be used to fill gaps in trim. Exposed fastener heads set, filled, and finished. Paint shall not bridge across separate trim members.\par {\b Drywall finish.}  Level 4 minimum throughout, Level 5 in raking light and gloss-paint conditions, ceilings under raking light, and all walls receiving sheen above eggshell.\par {\b Architect's right to reject.}  The architect may, at any time, reject work that does not conform to the contract documents -- including work shown on previously stamped shop drawings. Such rejected work shall be removed and replaced at no cost to the owner.\par \par {\b\fs26 08  Change Order Protocol}\par {\b Written change orders required.}  No change to the scope, quantity, material, finish, or method of any work shall be executed without a written change order signed by the owner and reviewed by the architect.\par {\b Reduce verbal to writing.}  Field directives from the owner do not constitute change orders; the contractor shall reduce any verbal request to a written proposal stating cost, schedule impact, and a single combined total, and shall not proceed until signed.\par {\b Risk for unauthorized work.}  Work performed without a signed change order is performed at the contractor's risk and expense. Substitutions of any kind require a change order.\par \par {\b\fs26 09  Quality Assurance & Final Inspection}\par {\b Required milestone reviews.}  A pre-pour foundation review, a pre-drywall walk-through (framing, MEP rough-in, waterproofing, flashing, blocking), and an outlet/switch walk-through with the owner -- held after framing and before any wall finishes -- are required and shall be scheduled with the architect with five working days' notice.\par {\b Substantial & final completion.}  The architect will conduct a substantial-completion walk and issue a punch list. All punch items shall be closed and re-verified before final payment.\par {\b Warranty walk.}  A one-year warranty walk shall be scheduled by the contractor at eleven months following substantial completion.\par {\b Limitation of architect's review.}  The architect's review, on site or through submittals, is for general conformance with the design intent only and does not relieve the contractor of sole responsibility for means, methods, sequencing, safety, or for the correctness and completeness of the work.\par \par {\b\fs26 10  Code Compliance -- Alabama Residential}\par {\b Applicable codes.}  All work shall comply with all codes, standards, and regulations in force in the project's jurisdiction at the time of permit, including without limitation the IRC, IECC, NEC, IPC, and IMC as adopted by the State of Alabama and the local authority having jurisdiction, and any local zoning, historic, or overlay-district requirements.\par {\b Stringency.}  Where this specification is more stringent than code, this specification governs. The contractor is responsible for code compliance and for obtaining all required inspections and certificates.\par \par }